\subsection{Testing the Bottom Condition for Fixed-Point Fusion}

The proof of bottom-started fixed-point fusion needs the two
iteration chains to agree at their first element: the map \(g\)
must send \(\bot_D\) to \(\bot_E\). Commutation then propagates
that equality at each step. Checking commutation without checking
the initial elements is insufficient, even if all maps are
continuous. Small tables are useful for testing the hypotheses
before turning a proposed identity into an optimization rule.

\textbf{Contract.} Given finite pointed posets \(D,E\) and
total tables for monotone \(f:D\to D\), \(h:E\to E\), and
\(g:D\to E\), report whether the bottom and commutation
hypotheses hold. If both hold, return the common result
\(g(\operatorname{lfp} f)=\operatorname{lfp} h\).
Monotonicity is a precondition, not checked by this routine.
On a finite poset it also implies preservation of nonempty
directed suprema, since every such directed set has a greatest
element.

\begin{sudocode}[Screen the hypotheses of a fusion rule]
\To{Commutation relates steps, not their starting states.}
\If{$g(\bot_D)\ne\bot_E$}
  \State \Return bottom condition failed
\EndIf
\For{each $x\in D$}
  \If{$g(f(x))\ne h(g(x))$}
    \State \Return commutation failed at $x$
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\To{Start at bottom to obtain the least fixed point.}
\Let{x}{\bot_D}
\While{$f(x)\ne x$}
  \Let{x}{f(x)}
\EndWhile
\State \Return $g(x)$
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Trace.} Set \(D=E=\{0<1\}\), \(f=h=\mathrm{id}\),
and \(g(x)=1\). Commutation holds at both values, but the routine
reports bottom condition failed. Indeed \(g(\operatorname{lfp} f)=1\)
while \(\operatorname{lfp} h=0\). In contrast, choosing
\(g=\mathrm{id}\) makes every check pass and returns \(0\).

\Cref{fig:visual-126} isolates the missing hypothesis: the two routes
from \(0_D\) agree, but neither route reaches the bottom of \(E\).

